1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,486
Look at this chart. Most of the time, price wanders inside a small range.

2
00:00:10,936 --> 00:00:14,174
And then suddenly, it runs off in one direction.

3
00:00:14,625 --> 00:00:18,177
Then at a new place, it starts wandering again.

4
00:00:18,628 --> 00:00:22,990
Why does this happen? Because the market is really an auction.

5
00:00:23,439 --> 00:00:26,601
Today we understand how this auction works.

6
00:00:27,601 --> 00:00:32,512
Hello, this is episode two of the SPIDER Order Flow series.

7
00:00:32,961 --> 00:00:37,951
Last time we learned: price is moved by whoever is in a hurry.

8
00:00:38,401 --> 00:00:47,152
Three questions today. How does the market work? What is a fair price? And why does price sometimes rest and sometimes run?

9
00:00:48,152 --> 00:00:52,907
First, picture a simple auction. An old painting is for sale.

10
00:00:53,357 --> 00:00:57,719
The auctioneer calls: 100 rupees. Someone raises a hand.

11
00:00:58,169 --> 00:01:03,655
So he raises the bid: 110, 120, 130.

12
00:01:04,105 --> 00:01:08,075
As long as hands keep going up, the bid keeps rising.

13
00:01:08,524 --> 00:01:12,809
At 150, no hand went up. The bidding stopped.

14
00:01:13,259 --> 00:01:17,857
So price rises for as long as it keeps finding buyers.

15
00:01:18,857 --> 00:01:22,645
The market is an auction too, but with one difference.

16
00:01:23,095 --> 00:01:27,457
Here, buyers and sellers are both bidding at the same time.

17
00:01:27,907 --> 00:01:33,705
Price goes up to find sellers. To ask: who will sell at this price?

18
00:01:34,155 --> 00:01:39,067
And it goes down to find buyers. Who will buy at this price?

19
00:01:39,517 --> 00:01:42,913
Where the other side shows up, a trade happens.

20
00:01:43,363 --> 00:01:48,118
So every price move is really a question the market is asking.

21
00:01:49,118 --> 00:01:53,637
Say price went up, and there sellers began selling hard.

22
00:01:54,086 --> 00:01:59,571
Answer received: buyers are scarce this high. Price comes back.

23
00:02:00,021 --> 00:02:03,809
Now price went down, and there buyers rushed in.

24
00:02:04,259 --> 00:02:09,668
Answer again: sellers are scarce this low. Price goes back up.

25
00:02:10,118 --> 00:02:14,323
Through this up-and-down testing, the market finds the right price.

26
00:02:15,323 --> 00:02:18,641
Now the most important word: fair price.

27
00:02:19,091 --> 00:02:24,341
A fair price is one that both buyer and seller find acceptable.

28
00:02:24,791 --> 00:02:28,840
At such a price, both sides trade comfortably.

29
00:02:29,290 --> 00:02:32,765
That is why the most trading happens at the fair price.

30
00:02:33,215 --> 00:02:40,137
Count how much traded at each price, out to the side like this. The middle grows the thickest.

31
00:02:40,587 --> 00:02:48,398
This thick zone is called value. Its full tool, Volume Profile, comes in episode 7.

32
00:02:49,398 --> 00:02:53,839
Now think the other way. What if price goes far above value?

33
00:02:54,289 --> 00:02:58,494
Buyers feel: too expensive. They stop.

34
00:02:58,944 --> 00:03:02,810
And sellers feel: great price, sell.

35
00:03:03,260 --> 00:03:09,373
This is an unfair price. Little trades here, and price does not stay long.

36
00:03:09,823 --> 00:03:16,013
Same below. If it is too cheap, sellers stop and buyers buy.

37
00:03:16,464 --> 00:03:23,308
That is why long tails form at the extremes, and price returns toward value quickly.

38
00:03:24,308 --> 00:03:29,061
Let us go to the market. At 6 in the morning, a truck of tomatoes arrives.

39
00:03:29,512 --> 00:03:32,438
The first price is 40 rupees a kilo.

40
00:03:32,888 --> 00:03:38,296
Hotel buyers are in a hurry and start buying. The price goes up to 44.

41
00:03:38,745 --> 00:03:44,623
At 44, ordinary customers stop. Too expensive. The price drops back to 42.

42
00:03:45,073 --> 00:03:51,107
By afternoon, most deals happened between 40 and 42. That is the value for the day.

43
00:03:51,557 --> 00:03:57,043
Now it is evening. The shopkeeper has stock left, and it will spoil by tomorrow.

44
00:03:57,493 --> 00:04:04,102
Now the seller is in a hurry. He drops the price: 36, 34, 32.

45
00:04:04,552 --> 00:04:10,821
At 32 new customers arrive and the stock sells. Price went down and found buyers.

46
00:04:11,821 --> 00:04:17,777
When buyers and sellers both agree on a price, that state is called balance.

47
00:04:18,227 --> 00:04:23,791
In balance, price wanders inside a range. On the chart it looks sideways.

48
00:04:24,241 --> 00:04:29,570
At the top edge, buyers find it expensive, so price returns down.

49
00:04:30,020 --> 00:04:35,349
At the bottom edge, sellers find it cheap, so price returns up.

50
00:04:35,799 --> 00:04:39,927
In the middle, at value, the most trading happens.

51
00:04:40,377 --> 00:04:43,694
The market spends most of its time in this balance.

52
00:04:44,694 --> 00:04:49,840
Now say big news arrives. Suddenly one side is in a big hurry.

53
00:04:50,290 --> 00:04:58,179
The old fair price is not fair anymore. Buyers look for sellers at the old price, but find none.

54
00:04:58,629 --> 00:05:02,913
So price has to move up, searching for new sellers.

55
00:05:03,363 --> 00:05:08,431
This state is called imbalance. On the chart it looks like a trend.

56
00:05:08,881 --> 00:05:15,647
In imbalance price moves fast, because the prices along the way are unfair, and nobody wants to stop there.

57
00:05:16,097 --> 00:05:22,131
Then at a new price both sides agree again. A new balance forms.

58
00:05:23,131 --> 00:05:25,639
So this is the full cycle of the market.

59
00:05:26,089 --> 00:05:31,340
Balance, then imbalance, then a new balance, then imbalance.

60
00:05:31,790 --> 00:05:36,544
Like breathing in and out. Rest, then move.

61
00:05:36,994 --> 00:05:41,043
The chart we saw at the start was just this cycle.

62
00:05:41,493 --> 00:05:46,561
Once you get this, you start seeing on the chart which state the market is in now.

63
00:05:47,561 --> 00:05:52,237
One question remains. Why does price spend so much time at value?

64
00:05:52,687 --> 00:05:58,799
Because both sides are present at value. At every price someone wants to deal.

65
00:05:59,249 --> 00:06:05,048
So trades happen slowly and calmly. Price has no need to run anywhere.

66
00:06:05,498 --> 00:06:11,611
But at unfair prices one side is missing. There price has to move on quickly.

67
00:06:12,061 --> 00:06:18,670
That is why the chart shows many candles near value, and only a few at unfair prices.

68
00:06:19,670 --> 00:06:25,704
Now something useful. When price leaves balance, only two things can happen.

69
00:06:26,154 --> 00:06:33,704
One: price goes outside, spends time there, and trading builds up. This is called acceptance.

70
00:06:34,154 --> 00:06:39,874
Acceptance means the market accepted the new price. A new balance is forming.

71
00:06:40,324 --> 00:06:48,031
Two: price goes outside, finds no partners, and comes back inside quickly. This is called rejection.

72
00:06:48,481 --> 00:06:54,828
Rejection means the outside price was unfair. The old balance is still alive.

73
00:06:55,278 --> 00:07:00,686
Deeper versions of this, like the failed auction, come in episode 20.

74
00:07:01,686 --> 00:07:04,455
So where does Order Flow fit in?

75
00:07:04,905 --> 00:07:12,297
The auction tells us what the market is searching for. Order Flow tells us who was in a hurry during the search.

76
00:07:12,747 --> 00:07:19,095
Whether buyers are tiring at the edge or pushing harder shows up in Order Flow tools.

77
00:07:19,545 --> 00:07:22,863
All of this in the coming episodes, one by one.

78
00:07:23,863 --> 00:07:25,665
Let us recap today.

79
00:07:26,115 --> 00:07:35,101
One: the market is an auction. Price goes up to find sellers and down to find buyers.

80
00:07:35,551 --> 00:07:44,067
Two: the fair price gets the most trading and the most time. At unfair prices, price does not stay.

81
00:07:44,517 --> 00:07:53,503
Three: balance means sideways, imbalance means trend. The market cycles between the two.

82
00:07:54,503 --> 00:08:04,299
Next episode: Bid, Ask, Spread and order types. What market, limit and stop orders really do.

83
00:08:04,749 --> 00:08:10,470
If this was useful, subscribe, and save this series playlist.

84
00:08:10,920 --> 00:08:17,033
For trade setups, our Telegram and Instagram are in the description. See you in the next episode.

